## Yes – you can draw contour lines **directly from a `raster-dem` source at runtime**  
MapLibre GL JS does not have a built-in “contour” layer type, but the **`maplibre-contour`** library does the heavy lifting for you:

* It reads the same DEM tiles you already use for hillshade / 3-D terrain.  
* In a Web-Worker it runs a marching-squares / contour-generation algorithm.  
* The result is exposed as a **virtual vector source** that you can style with ordinary `line` (or `symbol`) layers.

Below is a step-by-step recipe, plus tips for common gotchas.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the library

```bash
# npm / yarn / pnpm
npm install maplibre-contour
# or, if you prefer a CDN:
# <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.min.js"></script>
```

> **Why a worker?**  
> Generating contours for every tile can be CPU-intensive. `maplibre-contour` ships a tiny Web-Worker that runs off the main thread, keeping the map responsive.

---

## 2️⃣ Create a `DemSource` that knows how to read your DEM tiles

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';

// 1️⃣  Add the DEM source that you already use for terrain / hillshade
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp', // <-- your DEM tiles
  tileSize: 256,
  // The encoding must match the DEM you are serving.
  // Common values: 'terrarium', 'mapbox', 'raw' (RGB-packed)
  // If you are not sure, check the tile provider’s docs.
  // Example for Terrarium:
  // encoding: 'terrarium',
});

 // 2️⃣  Wrap that source with a DemSource that can generate contours
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  // Same URL pattern you used above – the library will request the same tiles.
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
  // Must match the DEM encoding (see above)
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  // The highest zoom you expect to request. 12-14 is typical for continent-scale maps.
  maxzoom: 12,
  // Set to true to spin up a dedicated worker (recommended)
  worker: true,
});
```

### What the options mean

| Option | What it does | Typical value |
|--------|--------------|---------------|
| `url` | Tile URL template for the DEM | `"https://…/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp"` |
| `encoding` | How elevation is packed in the RGB values (`'terrarium'`, `'mapbox'`, `'raw'`) | match your source |
| `maxzoom` | Highest zoom you will request (the worker will only fetch tiles up to this level) | `12`-`14` |
| `worker` | Use a Web-Worker (default `true`) | `true` |

---

## 3️⃣ Register the virtual vector source with MapLibre

`maplibre-contour` creates a **custom protocol** (`contour://`) that returns on-the-fly vector tiles.  
You just add a normal `vector` source that points to that protocol.

```js
// Tell the library about the map instance (adds the protocol handler)
demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);

// Add a *virtual* vector source that will serve the generated contours
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The URL is a *protocol* that the library resolves.
  // You can pass options here to control contour spacing, attribute names, etc.
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      // ── Contour spacing (in metres) per zoom level
      //   key = zoom, value = [minorInterval, majorInterval]
      thresholds: {
        // At zoom 10 show 200 m minor, 1000 m major lines
        10: [200, 1000],
        // At zoom 12 tighten the spacing
        12: [100, 500],
        // At zoom 14 show very fine detail
        14: [50, 200],
      },
      // Name of the property that will hold the elevation value (meters)
      elevationKey: 'ele',
      // Name of the property that marks a “major” contour (used for thicker lines or labels)
      levelKey: 'level',
      // Optional: give the source a stable layer name (default = "contours")
      contourLayer: 'contours',
    })
  ],
  // The vector source must know its maxzoom (same as the DEM source)
  maxzoom: 15,
});
```

**Result:**  
When the map asks for a tile like `contour://…/10/512/384.pbf`, the worker reads the underlying DEM tiles, runs the contour algorithm, and returns a Mapbox Vector Tile (MVT) with a single layer (`contours`) containing:

| Feature property | Meaning |
|------------------|---------|
| `ele`            | Elevation of the contour line (meters) |
| `level`          | `1` for a *major* contour, `0` for a *minor* one (as defined by `thresholds`) |

---

## 4️⃣ Style the contour lines

Now you can add a normal `line` layer (or a `symbol` layer for labels) that reads from the virtual source.

```js
// Minor (thin) contour lines
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-minor',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 0],   // only minor lines
  paint: {
    'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.4)',
    // Width can be a function of zoom if you like
    'line-width': 0.8,
  },
});

// Major (thicker) contour lines
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-major',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],   // only major lines
  paint: {
    'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.7)',
    'line-width': [
      'interpolate',
      ['linear'],
      ['zoom'],
      10, 1.2,
      14, 2.0
    ],
  },
});
```

### Optional: add labels

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1], // label only major contours
  layout: {
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular'],
    'text-size': 10,
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-ignore-placement': false,
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#222',
    'text-halo-color': '#fff',
    'text-halo-width': 1,
  },
});
```

---

## 5️⃣ Put it all together (minimal working example)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Runtime Contours</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.min.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},
      layers: []   // we’ll add everything programmatically
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 45,
    bearing: -17
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Add the DEM source (used for terrain, hillshade, etc.)
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
      tileSize: 256,
      // encoding must match the tiles you serve
      // encoding: 'terrarium',
    });

    // 2️⃣ Enable 3-D terrain (optional, but nice to see the relief)
    map.setTerrain({source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1.5});

    // 3️⃣ Set up the contour generator
    const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
      url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
      encoding: 'terrarium',
      maxzoom: 12,
      worker: true,
    });
    demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);

    // 4️⃣ Add the virtual vector source
    map.addSource('contours', {
      type: 'vector',
      tiles: [
        demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
          thresholds: {10: [200, 1000], 12: [100, 500], 14: [50, 200]},
          elevationKey: 'ele',
          levelKey: 'level',
          contourLayer: 'contours',
        })
      ],
      maxzoom: 15,
    });

    // 5️⃣ Style the contour lines (minor + major)
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'contour-minor',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'contours',
      'source-layer': 'contours',
      filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 0],
      paint: {
        'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.4)',
        'line-width': 0.8,
      },
    });

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'contour-major',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'contours',
      'source-layer': 'contours',
      filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
      paint: {
        'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.8)',
        'line-width': [
          'interpolate',
          ['linear'],
          ['zoom'],
          10, 1.2,
          14, 2.2,
        ],
      },
    });

    // 6️⃣ (Optional) add labels
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'contour-labels',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'contours',
      'source-layer': 'contours',
      filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
      layout: {
        'symbol-placement': 'line',
        'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
        'text-size': 10,
        'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular'],
      },
      paint: {
        'text-color': '#222',
        'text-halo-color': '#fff',
        'text-halo-width': 1,
      },
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page, tilt the map, and you’ll see contour lines appear **without any pre-generated vector tiles**.

---

## 📌 Key Take-aways & Common Pitfalls

| Issue | Why it happens | Fix |
|-------|----------------|-----|
| **No contours appear** | The DEM `encoding` does not match what you passed to `DemSource`. | Verify the provider’s docs (`terrarium`, `mapbox`, `raw`). If you’re unsure, inspect a tile’s RGB values or use `map.queryRenderedFeatures` on a `raster-dem` layer to see the raw values. |
| **Contours look jagged / missing at high zoom** | `maxzoom` of the `DemSource` is too low, so the worker never fetches higher-resolution DEM tiles. | Raise `maxzoom` to at least the highest zoom you intend to display (e.g., 14). |
| **Performance stalls when panning fast** | The worker is still generating tiles for newly-visible areas. | - Keep `thresholds` coarse at low zooms (larger intervals). <br> - Enable `worker: true` (default) – it already runs off-main-thread. <br> - Consider throttling map movement (`map.dragRotate`, `map.touchZoomRotate`) while the worker is busy, or pre-fetch a few tiles around the viewport. |
| **Contour colors don’t match the map style** | You’re styling the wrong `level` (minor vs. major) or using an opaque color that hides the underlying imagery. | Use `

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** recipe that turns the empty “page-background” you see above the horizon into a real sky with a gentle haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All of the pieces are native MapLibre – no extra plugins are needed.

---

## 1. Prerequisites

| What you need | Why it matters |
|---------------|----------------|
| **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 2.0** (or GL JS 5.6+ in the MapLibre fork) | The `sky` object was added in this version. |
| **A `raster-dem` source** and `map.setTerrain({source:…,exaggeration:…})` | Fog only renders when 3-D terrain is active. |
| **Pitch ≠ 0** (you already have `pitch: 70`) | The sky is only visible when the camera is tilted. |

If you already have terrain and pitch, you’re good to go.

---

## 2. Add the sky (root-level style object)

You can **declare the sky in the style JSON** *or* call `map.setSky()` from JavaScript after the map has loaded.  
Both approaches produce the same result – pick the one you prefer.

### 2-A. Declaring the sky in the style JSON

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "terrain-dem": {
      "type": "raster-dem",
      "url": "https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json",
      "tileSize": 256,
      "maxzoom": 12
    }
    /* …your other sources… */
  },

  "terrain": {
    "source": "terrain-dem",
    "exaggeration": 1
  },

  "sky": {
    "sky-color": "#80c0ff",               // light blue of the zenith
    "sky-horizon-blend": 0.5,             // how quickly the blue fades to the horizon
    "horizon-color": "#ffffff",          // colour of the very edge of the sky
    "horizon-fog-blend": 0.5,            // blend between horizon colour and fog
    "fog-color": "#c0d8ff",               // colour of the haze that meets the ground
    "fog-ground-blend": 0.5,              // how far the fog reaches down the terrain
    "atmosphere-blend": 0.2               // optional – adds a subtle atmospheric glow
  },

  "layers": [
    /* your normal layers – hillshade, raster, vector, etc. */
  ]
}
```

> **Tip:** If you already have a `background` layer, give it a transparent colour (`"background-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0)"`) so the sky can be seen behind it.

### 2-B. Adding the sky at runtime with `map.setSky()`

```javascript
// 1️⃣  Create the map (terrain already enabled)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      'terrain-dem': {
        type: 'raster-dem',
        url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
        maxzoom: 12
      }
      // …other sources…
    },
    terrain: { source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 },
    layers: [
      // …your visual layers (hillshade, raster, etc.)…
    ]
  },
  center: [13.4, 52.5],
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 70,          // you already have this
  bearing: 0,
  antialias: true    // recommended for smooth terrain
});

// 2️⃣  When the style is ready, install the sky
map.on('style.load', () => {
  map.setSky({
    'sky-color': '#80c0ff',
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
    'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.5,
    // optional – adds a faint glow near the horizon
    'atmosphere-blend': 0.2
  });
});
```

Both approaches give you the same visual result; the **runtime version** is handy when you want to toggle the sky on/off or change its colours dynamically.

---

## 3. What each property does (quick cheat-sheet)

| Property | Typical range | Effect |
|----------|---------------|--------|
| `sky-color` | any CSS colour | Colour of the sky at the zenith (top of the view). |
| `sky-horizon-blend` | `0 … 1` | How quickly the zenith colour fades to the horizon colour. `0` = abrupt, `1` = very gradual. |
| `horizon-color` | any CSS colour | Colour exactly at the horizon line. |
| `horizon-fog-blend` | `0 … 1` | Blend between the horizon colour and the fog colour. |
| `fog-color` | any CSS colour | Colour of the haze that meets the terrain. |
| `fog-ground-blend` | `0 … 1` | How far the fog reaches down the terrain. `0` = fog only at the very edge, `1` = fog covers the whole ground. |
| `atmosphere-blend` | `0 … 1` (optional) | Adds a subtle atmospheric glow; useful for a “sunset” feel. |

Play with the numbers until the sky looks natural for your region. A good starting point is the values shown in the examples above.

---

## 4. Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Why it happens | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| **Sky stays black / page background** | `map.setTerrain()` not called **or** terrain source is missing. Fog only renders when terrain is active. | Verify `map.setTerrain({source:'terrain-dem'})` runs *before* `map.setSky()`. |
| **Sky appears but no fog** | `fog-color` is fully transparent or `fog-ground-blend` is `0`. | Give `fog-color` an opaque value (e.g. `rgba(200,200,255,0.6)`) and set `fog-ground-blend` > 0. |
| **Sky disappears after a style change** | `style.load` replaces the whole style, wiping the sky object. | Re-apply `map.setSky()` inside the `styledata` or `style.load` event each time you change the style. |
| **Sky looks “flat” (no gradient)** | `sky-horizon-blend` set to `0`. | Increase it to `0.4-0.7`. |
| **Fog looks too strong / covers the whole map** | `fog-ground-blend` close to `1`. | Lower it (e.g. `0.3-0.5`). |
| **The background colour of the map still shows** | You have a `background` layer with an opaque colour. | Change its colour to transparent (`"background-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0)"`). |

---

## 5. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Terrain + Sky + Fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      'terrain-dem': {
        type: 'raster-dem',
        url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
        maxzoom: 12
      },
      // add any imagery source you like, e.g. OSM raster:
      'osm': {
        type: 'raster',
        tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
        tileSize: 256,
        maxzoom: 19
      }
    },
    terrain: { source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 },
    layers: [
      { id: 'osm', type: 'raster', source: 'osm' }
      // you could also add a hillshade or color-relief layer here
    ]
  },
  center: [13.4, 52.5],
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 70,
  bearing: 0,
  antialias: true
});

map.on('style.load', () => {
  // Sky + fog
  map.setSky({
    'sky-color': '#80c0ff',
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
    'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.5,
    'atmosphere-blend': 0.2
  });
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser, tilt the map (or use the mouse wheel while holding **Shift**), and you’ll see:

* A blue-to-white gradient sky,
* A soft haze that meets the terrain at the horizon,
* The terrain itself (mountains, valleys, etc.) rendered in 3-D.

---

## 6. Going further (optional)

| Want … | How to achieve it |
|--------|-------------------|
| **Sun / moon position** | MapLibre does **not** support `sky-atmosphere-sun` or `sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity`. You’d need to simulate it with a custom shader or a background image. |
| **Dynamic sky colour based on time of day** | Call `map.setSky()` again whenever the time changes, swapping the colour values. |
| **Fog that thickens with distance** | Adjust `fog-ground-blend` on the fly (e.g., lower it when zoomed out). |
| **Night-time star field** | Add a full-screen `background` layer with a star-texture and set its opacity based on a “night” flag. |

---

### TL;DR

1. **Enable terrain** (`map.setTerrain({source:'terrain-dem'})`).  
2. **Add a sky object** – either in the style JSON (`"sky": { … }`) **or** via `map.setSky({ … })`.  
3. Use the properties `sky-color`, `sky-horizon-blend`, `horizon-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-color`, `fog-ground-blend` (and optionally `atmosphere-blend`) to get the colour gradient and haze you want.  
4. Make sure the map’s background layer is transparent so the sky can be seen.

That’s all you need to replace the blank page background with a proper sky and atmospheric fog. Happy mapping!

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** recipe for putting a pre-rendered shaded-relief JPEG tile set under all of your vector data (including label layers) in a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣  What you need to know

| What you have | How MapLibre treats it |
|---------------|------------------------|
| **JPEG raster tiles** (e.g. `https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg`) | A **`raster` source** → a **`raster` layer**. No DEM, no hillshade, no `color-relief`. |
| **Vector data + label layers** | Usually added as `fill`, `line`, `symbol`, … layers. They sit **above** any raster layer that appears earlier in the style’s layer stack. |
| **Desired order** | `raster-relief` (bottom) → all your vector layers → label (`symbol`) layers (top). |

> **Tip:** If you are using a style JSON that already contains a bunch of layers, you can insert the raster layer *before* the first vector layer (or before a known “reference” layer) so you never have to reorder everything later.

---

## 2️⃣  Minimal HTML / JS skeleton

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – shaded-relief basemap + vector data</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (any style you like)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      // a plain background so you see the raster if it fails to load
      background: { type: 'background', paint: { 'background-color': '#e0e0e0' } },
      // we’ll add the raster layer later, then the vector sources/layers
      sources: {},   // empty for now – we’ll fill it programmatically
      layers: []     // same – we’ll push layers in the right order
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  When the map is ready, add the raster source + layer
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    // ---- 2a️⃣  Add the raster-DEM source (but it’s just a raster!)
    map.addSource('relief-basemap', {
      type: 'raster',
      // 👉  Replace with your own tile URL template
      tiles: ['https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
      tileSize: 256,               // most JPEG/PNG tiles are 256 px
      // optional: if your tiles are in WebP or another format, just change the extension
      // optional: set maxzoom/minzoom if you know them
      // optional: set attribution
      attribution: '© Your-Provider'
    });

    // ---- 2b️⃣  Add the raster layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'relief-basemap',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'relief-basemap',
      // paint properties are optional – you can tweak opacity, fade, etc.
      paint: {
        // make it a little translucent if you want the vector fill to “show through”
        // 'raster-opacity': 0.95,
        // control how quickly new tiles fade in (ms). 0 = instant.
        // 'raster-fade-duration': 300
      }
    }, /* insertBefore */ firstVectorLayerId );

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Add your vector source(s) and layers
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // Example: a GeoJSON source with polygons + a label layer
    map.addSource('countries', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/datasets/geo-countries/master/data/countries.geojson'
    });

    // Fill layer (under the labels)
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'countries-fill',
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'countries',
      paint: {
        'fill-color': '#4a90e2',
        'fill-opacity': 0.4
      }
    });

    // Line layer (optional)
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'countries-outline',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'countries',
      paint: {
        'line-color': '#ffffff',
        'line-width': 1
      }
    });

    // Symbol (label) layer – **this will be on top**
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'countries-label',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'countries',
      layout: {
        'text-field': ['get', 'ADMIN'],
        'text-font': ['Open Sans Bold', 'Arial Unicode MS Bold'],
        'text-size': 12,
        'text-anchor': 'center'
      },
      paint: {
        'text-color': '#000000',
        'text-halo-color': '#ffffff',
        'text-halo-width': 1.5
      }
    });
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  Helper: find the first vector layer id (if you have a style JSON)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // If you are loading a full-blown style (e.g. from MapTiler, OSM, etc.) you may not know the
  // id of the first vector layer. The snippet below finds it automatically.
  function firstVectorLayerId() {
    const layers = map.getStyle().layers;
    for (const l of layers) {
      // All non-raster layer types are “vector” for our purpose
      if (['fill', 'line', 'symbol', 'circle', 'heatmap', 'fill-extrusion', 'hillshade', 'color-relief'].includes(l.type)) {
        return l.id;
      }
    }
    // Fallback – insert at the very top (will be above everything else)
    return undefined;
  }
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Create a blank style** | Starts with an empty `sources`/`layers` object so we can control ordering ourselves. |
| **Add a `raster` source** | `type: 'raster'` tells MapLibre “these tiles are images, not DEMs”. |
| **Add a `raster` layer** | The layer draws the JPEG tiles. The second argument to `addLayer` (`firstVectorLayerId`) tells MapLibre to insert the raster **below** the first vector layer that already exists (or at the bottom if none exist). |
| **Add vector sources & layers** | Anything you add *after* the raster layer will automatically sit on top. The label (`symbol`) layer is added last, guaranteeing it’s on top of everything else. |
| **Optional paint tweaks** | `raster-opacity`, `raster-fade-duration`, etc., let you fine-tune the look. |

---

## 3️⃣  Quick checklist for a production-ready implementation

| ✅ Item | Why it matters |
|--------|----------------|
| **Correct tile URL template** (`{z}/{x}/{y}`) | MapLibre will request the exact tiles you host. |
| **`tileSize` matches your tiles** (usually 256) | Prevents blurry scaling. |
| **`maxzoom` / `minzoom` set** (if you know them) | Stops the map from requesting tiles that don’t exist. |
| **Attribution** (`attribution` field on the source) | Legal compliance & good practice. |
| **Layer ordering** – raster added *before* any vector layers | Guarantees the basemap stays at the bottom. |
| **`raster-fade-duration: 0`** (optional) | Removes the default 300 ms fade-in if you want instant tile swaps (useful for low-latency UI). |
| **CORS / Access-Control-Allow-Origin** on your tile server | Otherwise the browser will block the tiles. |
| **Cache-control headers** on the tile server | Improves performance on repeat visits. |
| **Responsive design** – keep the map container at `width:100%; height:100%` | So the basemap fills the screen on any device. |

---

## 4️⃣  Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Tiles appear gray / missing** | CORS error or wrong URL template. | Open the browser console → look for `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` errors. Serve tiles with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). |
| **Raster layer covers vector data** | Raster layer added *after* the vector layers (or `moveLayer` placed it on top). | Ensure `addLayer('relief-basemap', …, firstVectorLayerId)` or call `map.moveLayer('relief-basemap', 'countries-fill')` to push it down. |
| **Image looks too bright/dark** | JPEG was pre-shaded for a specific background (e.g., white). | Adjust `raster-brightness-min`, `raster-brightness-max`, or `raster-contrast` paint properties. |
| **Labels flicker when panning** | `raster-fade-duration` is non-zero and the map is constantly loading new tiles. | Set `'raster-fade-duration': 0` in the layer’s `paint`. |
| **Zoom levels beyond your tiles show blank space** | `maxzoom` not set, so MapLibre keeps requesting higher-zoom tiles that don’t exist. | Add `maxzoom: 12` (or whatever your highest zoom is) to the source definition. |
| **Map looks “flat” – you expected 3-D terrain** | You’re using a pre-rendered raster; it’s 2-D only. | If you need true 3-D terrain, you must use a `raster-dem` source + `map.setTerrain()`. The JPEG basemap cannot be turned into 3-D terrain. |

---

## 5️⃣  TL;DR – Minimal code snippet

If you already have a style (e.g. from MapTiler) and just want to prepend the raster basemap:

```js
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // 1️⃣  Add the raster source
  map.addSource('relief-basemap', {
    type: 'raster',
    tiles: ['https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
    tileSize: 256,
    attribution: '© Your-Provider'
  });

  // 2️⃣  Insert the raster layer *below* the first existing vector layer
  const firstVector = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => !['raster', 'background'].includes(l.type));
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'relief-basemap',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'relief-basemap',
    paint: { 'raster-fade-duration': 0 }
  }, firstVector?.id);   // `undefined` means “add on top”, but we give the id to force “below”
});
```

That’s it—your shaded-relief JPEG tiles now sit under every vector feature, and your label (`symbol`) layers stay on top.

---

### 🎉 You’re ready!

Just replace the placeholder URL with your own tile endpoint, adjust any optional paint properties you like, and you’ll have a beautiful, static shaded-relief basemap supporting all of your interactive vector data and labels. Happy mapping! 🚀
